The following is from a 2017 paper by cognitive psychologist Elena Marchetti.
Experiments measuring reaction times in bilingual speakers suggest that both languages are simultaneously active even when a speaker intends to use only one. In lexical-decision tasks, bilingual participants showed significant interference from cognates — words that share form and meaning across languages — indicating that the non-target language is not fully suppressed during single-language processing.

Text 2
The following is from a 2023 study by neurolinguist Kwame Asante.
Functional MRI data collected from trilingual participants reveal that the degree of cross-linguistic activation varies substantially depending on the typological distance between the languages involved. Participants whose three languages belonged to the same family showed far greater interference than participants whose languages spanned two or more unrelated families, suggesting that structural similarity modulates the co-activation effect.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 identifies a variable that refines the phenomenon described in Text 1."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 provides evidence that directly contradicts the central finding of Text 1."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 applies the methodology described in Text 1 to a substantially larger participant sample."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 argues that cross-linguistic interference occurs only in bilingual, not trilingual, speakers."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 1 establishes that both languages are co-activated in bilingual speakers. Text 2 does not dispute this finding but adds nuance by showing that the degree of co-activation depends on typological distance between languages. This makes Text 2 a refinement — identifying a moderating variable — rather than a rebuttal. The option claiming direct contradiction misreads Text 2, which builds on, rather than opposes, the co-activation finding. The option about methodology is inaccurate: Text 1 uses reaction-time tasks while Text 2 uses fMRI, so the methods differ. The option claiming interference occurs only in bilinguals inverts Text 2''s point; Text 2 confirms interference exists in trilinguals as well, varying by language relatedness.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);









- Urban beekeeping has expanded rapidly in cities such as Detroit, Portland, and Philadelphia since 2018.
- A 2022 survey by the Urban Agriculture Consortium found that small bakeries and restaurants located within two miles of rooftop apiaries saved an average of \\$4,700 per year on honey procurement costs.
- Rooftop hives also support pollination of community gardens.
- Some cities require permits and inspections for rooftop hives, adding regulatory costs for beekeepers.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Urban beekeeping has grown in Detroit, Portland, Philadelphia, and other cities, where it supports community garden pollination and requires permits."},{"key":"B","text":"According to a 2022 Urban Agriculture Consortium survey, small bakeries and restaurants near rooftop apiaries saved roughly \\$4,700 annually on honey costs, illustrating a concrete economic advantage of urban beekeeping."},{"key":"C","text":"Although urban beekeeping has expanded rapidly since 2018, some cities impose permit and inspection requirements that add regulatory costs for beekeepers who maintain rooftop hives."},{"key":"D","text":"Rooftop hives support the pollination of community gardens in cities like Detroit, Portland, and Philadelphia, contributing to broader urban agricultural efforts that began expanding around 2018."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to emphasize the economic benefit of urban beekeeping to local businesses. The option beginning "According to a 2022 Urban Agriculture Consortium survey" directly cites the \\$4,700 annual savings for bakeries and restaurants and frames this as a concrete economic advantage, fulfilling the stated rhetorical purpose. The option mentioning growth in Detroit, Portland, and Philadelphia along with permits combines general expansion facts with regulatory details but omits any economic benefit. The option focusing on permit and inspection requirements emphasizes regulatory costs rather than economic benefits. The option about pollination of community gardens highlights an ecological benefit rather than an economic one for local businesses.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

- Dr. Tomoko Sato is a geochemist at the University of Iceland.
- Volcanic soil is widely assumed to be uniformly rich in nutrients that promote plant growth.
- In a 2023 study, Sato analyzed soil samples from 38 sites on basaltic lava fields in eastern Iceland that had erupted between 1724 and 1783.
- Sato found that phosphorus levels varied by a factor of nine across the 38 sites, with some sites registering phosphorus concentrations too low to sustain most native grass species.
- The variation correlated strongly with the cooling rate of the original lava flow, not with the age of the soil.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"University of Iceland geochemist Dr. Tomoko Sato collected soil from 38 basaltic lava sites in eastern Iceland that had erupted between 1724 and 1783 and measured their phosphorus content as part of a 2023 study on volcanic soil nutrients."},{"key":"B","text":"Volcanic soil has long been assumed to be uniformly nutrient-rich, but a 2023 study by geochemist Dr. Tomoko Sato found that phosphorus levels in basaltic lava fields varied ninefold across 38 Icelandic sites, with some sites too phosphorus-poor to sustain native grasses."},{"key":"C","text":"According to a 2023 study, phosphorus concentrations in Icelandic volcanic soil correlate with the cooling rate of lava rather than with soil age, a finding by Dr. Tomoko Sato that has implications for understanding nutrient distribution in volcanic regions."},{"key":"D","text":"Dr. Tomoko Sato, a geochemist at the University of Iceland, studies basaltic lava fields in eastern Iceland, where eruptions occurred between 1724 and 1783 and where soil samples reveal varying phosphorus levels."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to present a finding that challenges a commonly held assumption about volcanic soil. The option beginning "Volcanic soil has long been assumed" explicitly states the assumption of uniform nutrient richness and then directly contrasts it with Sato''s finding of ninefold phosphorus variation, including sites too poor for native grasses, thereby fulfilling the rhetorical purpose. The option beginning "University of Iceland geochemist" describes Sato''s methodology without identifying the assumption being challenged. The option beginning "According to a 2023 study" presents the cooling-rate correlation, which is a secondary finding; it does not frame the result as challenging the assumption of uniform nutrient richness. The option beginning "Dr. Tomoko Sato, a geochemist" provides biographical and contextual details without articulating either the assumption or the challenge to it.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





The following is from a 2019 essay by historian of science Beatrice Fontaine.
The replication crisis in social psychology has exposed a fundamental methodological weakness: many landmark findings were established through small-sample experiments that capitalized on statistical noise. Fontaine argues that the discipline must prioritize large-scale preregistered replications over novel, attention-grabbing studies. Only by systematically confirming or disconfirming existing claims can the field establish a reliable empirical foundation.

Text 2
The following is from a 2022 commentary by experimental psychologist Andrei Volkov.
Recent large-scale replication projects, while valuable, risk creating a culture of methodological conservatism in which researchers allocate disproportionate resources to re-testing established findings at the expense of exploring genuinely new phenomena. Volkov contends that the field''s long-term vitality depends on maintaining a balance between confirmatory rigor and the exploratory work that generates novel hypotheses worth confirming.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"As a perspective that underestimates the severity of the replication crisis by diverting resources away from the confirmatory work needed to rebuild the field's credibility"},{"key":"B","text":"As a reasonable compromise that effectively resolves the tension between confirmatory and exploratory research methods"},{"key":"C","text":"As an endorsement of the same small-sample methodology that originally produced unreliable findings in social psychology"},{"key":"D","text":"As a proposal that would improve replication efforts by incorporating exploratory hypotheses into preregistered study designs"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 1 argues that the discipline must prioritize large-scale replications to address the replication crisis, framing confirmatory work as essential to rebuilding a reliable foundation. Text 2 cautions against allocating disproportionate resources to replication at the expense of exploration. From Fontaine''s perspective, Volkov''s concern about methodological conservatism would likely appear to underestimate the crisis''s severity by pulling resources toward novel work before the empirical foundation is secured. The option describing a reasonable compromise attributes an approving stance that conflicts with Fontaine''s strong prioritization of replication. The option about endorsing small-sample methods mischaracterizes Text 2, which does not advocate for small samples but for a balance between replication and exploration. The option about incorporating exploratory hypotheses into preregistered designs describes a specific methodological proposal that neither text actually discusses.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 120, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);






- Wildlife ecologist Dr. Ramesh Anand advocates rewilding, reintroducing apex predators to restore ecosystem balance.
- Conservation biologist Dr. Priya Naik favors habitat corridor construction, connecting fragmented reserves to enable gene flow among isolated populations.
- Both researchers published papers in 2024 in the journal Conservation Biology.
- In their respective papers, Anand and Naik each independently concluded that long-term success of any conservation initiative in South Asian grasslands depends on securing formal grazing agreements with pastoral communities whose livestock share the landscape.
- Anand''s rewilding pilot in Gujarat showed a 34 percent increase in native herbivore populations over three years.
- Naik''s corridor project in Karnataka documented gene flow resumption between two previously isolated populations of Indian wolves.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"While Dr. Ramesh Anand's rewilding pilot in Gujarat increased native herbivore populations by 34 percent, Dr. Priya Naik's corridor project in Karnataka restored gene flow between isolated Indian wolf populations, and both results were published in Conservation Biology in 2024."},{"key":"B","text":"Despite advocating different strategies, both Dr. Ramesh Anand and Dr. Priya Naik independently concluded in 2024 Conservation Biology papers that conservation efforts in South Asian grasslands ultimately require formal grazing agreements with the pastoral communities that share the land."},{"key":"C","text":"Dr. Ramesh Anand, who supports reintroducing apex predators to restore ecosystem balance, and Dr. Priya Naik, who favors building habitat corridors to promote gene flow, each published findings in Conservation Biology in 2024 about conservation in South Asian grasslands."},{"key":"D","text":"In 2024, conservation researchers published competing proposals in Conservation Biology: Dr. Anand argued for rewilding through apex predator reintroduction, while Dr. Naik championed habitat corridor construction to reduce genetic isolation among fragmented wildlife populations."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to highlight a point of agreement between the two researchers despite their differing approaches. The option beginning "Despite advocating different strategies" explicitly acknowledges the difference in methods and then identifies the specific shared conclusion, that conservation in South Asian grasslands requires formal grazing agreements with pastoral communities, directly fulfilling the rhetorical purpose. The option beginning "While Dr. Ramesh Anand''s rewilding pilot" presents each researcher''s individual results side by side without identifying any point of agreement between them. The option beginning "Dr. Ramesh Anand, who supports" summarizes each researcher''s approach and notes they both published in the same journal, but sharing a publication venue is not a substantive point of agreement on conservation strategy. The option beginning "In 2024, conservation researchers" frames the two proposals as competing, emphasizing disagreement rather than convergence, and omits the shared conclusion about grazing agreements entirely.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 120, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

The following is from a 2018 report by agricultural economist Miriam Tegegne.
Smallholder farmers in sub-Saharan Africa who adopt drought-resistant crop varieties consistently achieve higher yields than those who plant traditional cultivars, even during seasons of below-average rainfall. This yield advantage suggests that the widespread distribution of improved varieties should be a central pillar of regional food-security policy.

Text 2
The following is from a 2021 commentary by rural sociologist David Ochieng.
While drought-resistant varieties may offer yield benefits under controlled conditions, their adoption depends on factors that purely agronomic analyses tend to overlook. Seed cost, local soil compatibility, and farmers'' established cultural preferences for certain grain textures and flavors frequently determine whether an improved variety is planted at all.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that drought-resistant varieties improve yields but arguing that adoption depends on non-agronomic factors"},{"key":"B","text":"By rejecting the claim that drought-resistant varieties produce any measurable yield advantage"},{"key":"C","text":"By proposing that traditional cultivars should be entirely replaced by genetically modified organisms"},{"key":"D","text":"By asserting that food-security policy should focus exclusively on improving irrigation infrastructure"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 2 acknowledges that drought-resistant varieties "may offer yield benefits" but stresses that non-agronomic factors — seed cost, soil compatibility, and cultural preferences — determine whether farmers actually adopt them. This response accepts the yield claim while arguing that adoption barriers complicate the policy recommendation. The option claiming rejection of yield advantages contradicts Text 2, which concedes the yield benefit. The option about genetically modified organisms introduces a topic neither text discusses. The option about focusing exclusively on irrigation invents a policy position that Text 2 does not advocate.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



